course: arts
lesson: 26
title: Age ratings, scary films and choosing what to watch
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Films get an age rating, a label like U, PG, 12A or 15 in the UK, or G, PG or PG-13 in the US, that warns families what is inside: how scary, how violent, how rude.
explain: A rating is not there to annoy kids; it is like the depth sign at a swimming pool, telling you where you can stand and where it is too deep for now.
explain: Picture a sleepover: everyone says a horror film "isn't even that scary", then one friend cannot sleep for three nights; scary pictures can stick in the mind, and saying no is allowed.
explain: Choosing well also means picking something instead of scrolling for ever, putting the phone down to catch the clues, and not spoiling the ending for others.
explain: People say it their own way: "shook" means badly scared, "a lightweight" is someone who tires easily, and "daylight robbery" means far too expensive.
fact: In the UK films are rated U, PG, 12A, 15 and 18, and in the US G, PG, PG-13, R and NC-17; the ratings warn about scary scenes, violence and bad language.
fact: Parents decide what children watch at home, and cinemas must follow the age rules.
fact: Ratings look at much more than swearing: a frightening or violent scene can matter more than a rude word.
fact: Scary films can cause bad dreams, especially for young children; talking about it, watching something funny after and remembering it is made up all help.
fact: Streaming services have huge libraries, and too much choice can make people scroll for ages without choosing.
fact: Family film nights work best when the film is not too long or too late and everyone gets a turn to choose.
fact: Critics and review sites give scores, but those are opinions, and your own taste may differ.
fact: Films hide clues early on, so scrolling on a phone during a film means missing details that matter later.
fact: A spoiler is telling someone a surprise in a story before they see it; people argue whether it ruins the film, but most find it annoying.
fact: Cinemas earn a big part of their money from snacks, because much of the ticket money goes to the film company.
fact: Documentaries are films about real life, like nature, history or sport, and the best ones are as gripping as any story.
exercise: Ollie's mum says no to a 15-rated horror film. What is a rating for? | To warn families what a film holds | To make films sound more exciting so that more kids want to see them | To stop grown-ups from watching films that are made for children | To say how long it lasts
exercise: Zara cannot sleep after a scary film. What helps? | Talking about it, then something funny | Watching an even scarier film straight away to toughen herself up | Keeping it a secret from everyone so nobody thinks she is a baby | Staying up all night
exercise: Ivan's grandad says now there are "a thousand films on your phone". What can too much choice cause? | Endless scrolling | Watching every single film in the library in just one night | Phones breaking down because they hold far too many films | Films getting shorter and shorter so that more of them fit
exercise: Why does Dad always fall asleep at film night? | Late films can make anyone sleepy | Dads are not allowed to watch films | The film is always a cartoon | Sofas are made to make dads sleep
exercise: Danny reads that a film is "rotten" but he wanted to see it. What is wise? | Read why, then decide himself | Never watch any film that has a low score on a review website | Only ever watch the films that the critics hate the most | Ask for his money back
exercise: Leah missed the twist because she was on her phone. Why? | Films hide clues that you miss if you look away | Phones change the film's ending | The twist only happens if nobody is scrolling | Films are made to be watched only half the time
exercise: Ahmed's cousin told him the ending. What is that called? | A spoiler | A rating | A trailer | A review
exercise: Holly's dad says "it's about the content". What do ratings look at? | Scary and violent scenes too | Only the number of rude words that are said in the whole film | Only how long the film is and how late at night it finishes | Only the music
exercise: Why is cinema popcorn so expensive? | Snacks earn cinemas lots of money | Cinema popcorn is made from a very rare kind of corn from abroad | The film company sets the snack prices along with the ticket prices | Actors are paid from it
exercise: Kalani loves an ocean documentary. What is a documentary? | A film about real life | A cartoon made specially for school lessons about the sea | A film that was made without using any camera at all | A film that only shows maps and charts with a voice reading facts
exercise: Which is a UK age rating? | 12A | R | G | NC-17
exercise: Who decides what a child watches at home? | Their parents or carers | The child's friends | The cinema staff | The film's actors
exercise: Do people agree that spoilers ruin films? | It is argued | Yes, every scientist in the world agrees that spoilers ruin films | No, everybody loves spoilers and asks for them before every film | They are illegal
exercise: How can a family make film night fair? | Take turns choosing | Let the oldest person in the family always choose every film | Always pick the longest film so everyone gets their money's worth | Choose the film by seeing who can shout the loudest
exercise: Which is true about critics' scores? | They are opinions | They are facts that cannot be wrong, because critics are experts | They tell you exactly how much you yourself will like the film | They are made up by the cinema to sell more tickets to people
test: Your mate says, "It's 15 but it's not even that scary, ya wimp." What is the best answer? | Ratings warn about content; saying no is fine | Ratings are just there to annoy kids and do not mean anything | Only babies follow ratings, so you should watch it to show you are not one | Mates watched it, so it is safe
test: What would happen if you spent all film night scrolling the menu? | No time left to watch | You would end up seeing more films than ever before in one night | The streaming service would get annoyed and choose a film for you | The films in the library would all become much shorter
test: A Gen Z kid says, "Critics said it's trash, so it's trash, period" ("period" means end of discussion). What is fair? | You can read their reasons and still disagree | Critics are always right about every film because it is their job | Critics never actually watch the films they write reviews about | Critics are always right
test: Your cousin from Leeds goes, "Spoiler: the dog dies, soz" ("soz" means sorry). What would have been kind? | Keeping the ending secret | Telling everyone else the ending even faster so it is fair | Only spoiling the films that he hates, never the good ones | Waiting a day first
test: In a cartoon, a kid sneaks into an 18 film and says ratings are "only about swears". What is the mistake? | Ratings also cover scary and violent scenes | Ratings are only about how long films are | 18 films have no rude words at all | Ratings are only for cartoons
test: Gran says the popcorn is "daylight robbery". Why are cinema snacks so dear? | Snacks are how cinemas earn | Popcorn has to be flown in by plane from abroad every single day | The actors in the film get paid out of the popcorn money | Cinema snacks cost more because they are extra healthy
test: Your friend from Glasgow is "pure feart" ("feart" means scared) after a horror film. Which is a good thing to remind her? | It is made up, with actors, make-up and effects | Monsters are real but only at night | Being scared means she is a baby | She must watch it again to stop the fear
test: Which is a good reason to keep your phone away during a film? | You will catch the clues | Phones make films play faster, so you miss the end | The film stops playing if anyone's phone is switched on | Cinemas confiscate phones
test: Kalani's friend says documentaries are "boring school stuff". What is fair to say? | Many are gripping | All documentaries are boring because that is the rule for making them | Documentaries are only made for teachers to show in their lessons | Real life is never anywhere near as exciting as a cartoon
test: Your family argues about the film every week. What would most likely help? | Taking turns to choose | Never watching films again | Letting the dog choose | Watching three films at once
test: A kid says a PG film is fine because "it's got no swearing". What else should be checked? | Whether it has frightening or violent scenes | Whether the poster is colourful | Whether it was made in America | Whether the actors are famous
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the age rating attached to a film. | Guidance on its content | The augmentation of the film's appeal to children by the promise of excitement | The prohibition of all films to adult audiences unless accompanied by children | The duration of the film
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the revenue of cinemas from refreshments? | Refreshments constitute a substantial portion of the cinema's income | Refreshments are sold at cost without any profit | The price of refreshments is fixed by the actors | Refreshments are supplied free of charge with all tickets
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the disclosure of a narrative's conclusion. | Its effect is argued, though most find it annoying | It is universally agreed by researchers to enhance the enjoyment of all viewers | It is prohibited by statute in most jurisdictions and punished by a fine | It has no effect upon any viewer
exam: Ascertain the measure most likely to diminish distress following the viewing of a frightening film. | Discussion of the film and the subsequent viewing of something amusing | The immediate viewing of a more frightening film | The concealment of the distress from all persons | The avoidance of sleep for the entire night
